WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.900
Aliasing occurs when multiple pointers can refer to the same allocation.

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:06.600
This is common in C.

3
00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:09.500
A caller passes a pointer to a helper.

4
00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:12.320
A structure stores a pointer to data owned elsewhere.

5
00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:15.300
A cache records a pointer for faster lookup.

6
00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:17.880
A callback receives context.

7
00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:23.340
Each alias can be harmless when its lifetime rules are clear, and risky when they are not.

8
00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:28.580
Shared references complicate the question of who may free memory and when.

9
00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:34.440
If two components both believe they own the same allocation, repeated cleanup becomes likely.

10
00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:39.340
If neither component believes it owns the allocation, leaks become likely.

11
00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:44.480
If one component borrows but keeps the pointer too long, stale access becomes likely.

12
00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:48.840
Borrowed pointers should not outlive the object they reference.

13
00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:54.700
A borrowed pointer can be safe for a single function call or a clearly bounded operation.

14
00:00:54.800 --> 00:01:03.200
It becomes risky when the callee stores it, uses it asynchronously, or assumes the owner will remain alive without a documented guarantee.

15
00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:08.360
APIs should clearly distinguish owning references from non-owning references.

16
00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:11.660
Names can help, but behavior must be consistent.

17
00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:15.600
A function that takes ownership should say so explicitly.

18
00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:19.540
A function that only observes should not release or retain the pointer.

19
00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:25.000
A function that retains a pointer should state how long and under what conditions.

20
00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:30.720
Containers should document whether they own their elements or merely reference them.

21
00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:36.500
A list might free each element during destruction, or it might only store borrowed pointers.

22
00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:43.400
Both designs can be valid, but callers must know which one is in force before inserting pointers or destroying the container.

23
00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:46.720
Avoid hidden ownership side effects that surprise callers.

24
00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:53.860
If a function releases an argument, stores an argument, or invalidates a pointer, that behavior should be obvious from the contract.

25
00:01:53.960 --> 00:02:00.440
The safest ownership designs make pointer lifetime visible at the call site and easy to verify during review.
